From aa04297c59d48602da74901c09c8b0412939fb6c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PixelOS PIF <pif@localhost>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 16:14:06 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] key attestation: assert the hardware's KeyMint version and
 never sign a mismatched identity

Two independent reasons a software certificate failed DEVICE, both visible in the
2026-11-06 on-device log.

**The asserted version described the ROM, not the hardware.**
getAttestVersion() mapped the running SDK, so a PixelOS 17 build on KeyMint V3
hardware claimed attestationVersion=400 while the vendor HAL reports V3 (300) and
the spoofed Pixel 6a profile launched at API 32 (100). Verifiers cross-check the
claimed version against the attested key, so all three disagreeing is itself the
mismatch. Derive it from ro.product.first_api_level instead, the bootloader value
that PIF does not rewrite: this device reports 35 and therefore asserts 300, which
is also what the vendor HAL supports. On this hardware Evolution X's HAL probe and
its first-API fallback both land on 300, so the probe is not needed and is omitted;
it is blocked by SELinux inside an app process anyway.

The provider lookup delegated to the boot property can therefore be dropped rather
than duplicated in a second place.

**A missing profile silently became the handset identity.**
identityBytes() fell back to the process Build when the profile had no entry, so a
process whose Build was not spoofed produced a certificate claiming
OnePlus/PKX110 next to a Pixel fingerprint. That is exactly the contradiction this
code exists to remove. Return null instead, require BRAND/DEVICE/PRODUCT to agree
with the Build before treating the profile as live, and have callers keep the
untouched hardware path when no identity is available. The Keystore2 recovery
boundary already returned null on that condition, which preserves the original
error instead of installing a contradictory certificate.

The version mapping moved to AttestationVersion so the numbers this ROM asserts are
covered by host checks with real API levels rather than asserted through a stub.

Also from the same Evolution X generation:
  - build every spoofed key through the software path, including the leaf-rewrite
    case, because a generateKey reply that differs from the later getKeyEntry reply
    is itself a probe signal; the key is still imported, so storage stays
    hardware-backed and both reads agree;
  - keep a numeric Build.VERSION.RELEASE when the fingerprint says CANARY;
  - treat an all-zero boot key or hash as absent instead of signing it into
    RootOfTrust;
  - issue a random leaf serial per key, since a fixed serial is a fingerprint.

Normal-path diagnostic lines added for the 11-06 investigation are removed again;
a warning remains where the hardware path loses, and the asserted version is logged
once per process.

Change-Id: If96029568cf2b7d6a17a82753db232ac3e869b47
